 "upload": {
  "title": "Trump, Xi, Putin Sign a Peace Declaration. What's in It? #shorts",
  "description": "Donald Trump, Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin held their first three-way meeting on November 19, 2026, in Shenzhen, China, on the sidelines of the APEC summit. Afterwards the White House and China's Xinhua news agency published a joint \"Declaration toward Peace\". In it, the three leaders reaffirm that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought, and say their countries will hold a dialogue on reducing nuclear risks and preventing the spread of nuclear weapons. According to reports, the text sets no timetable and contains no commitment to cut nuclear arsenals. The declaration also commits the three countries to increase the production and supply of refined petroleum products, calls for an unspecified reform of the United Nations, and says the International Criminal Court and similar bodies must not abuse their jurisdiction. The ICC issued an arrest warrant for Vladimir Putin in March 2023. According to reports, the declaration does not mention the war in Ukraine. At a press conference in Shenzhen, Putin said that after the Ukrainian strikes on the Crimean Bridge, Russia will not negotiate a ceasefire, and threatened a response \"far more painful\" than the attack. Chinese state media called the talks positive and constructive; U.S. media reported few concrete results.\n\nSources:\nWhite House, Nov 19, 2026: U.S.-Russia-China Heads of State Declaration toward Peace\nXinhua via gov.cn, Nov 19, 2026: Full text: China-Russia-U.S. Heads of State Declaration toward Peace\nCBS News, Nov 19, 2026: Trump, Xi and Putin sit down for a rare 3-way meeting in China, but appear to achieve little\nCBC News, Nov 19, 2026: Xi-Trump-Putin statement warns world bodies not to 'abuse their jurisdiction'\nGulf News, Nov 19, 2026: 'No winners in nuclear war': Trump, Xi, Putin pledge dialogue after Shenzhen summit\nKyiv Post, Nov 19, 2026: Putin, Trump, Xi Joint Statement on Nuclear Talks, UN Reform, No Mention of Ukraine\nAsia Times, Nov 2026: Shenzhen summit letdown and the limits of multipolarity\nDeccan Herald, Nov 19, 2026: After bridge attack by Ukraine, Putin says he will not negotiate ceasefire, vows retaliation\n\n#geopolitics #Trump #XiJinping #Putin #nuclear",
